Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Corrected Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  29 REMOVED_MONTH 96 ACCESSIONED ON  29 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 10 57 CLINICAL DATA  60 year old female with left breast axillary mass                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE 2  REMOVED_DATE CM  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  SEE NOTE  LYMPH NODES  LEFT AXILLA   METASTATIC CARCINOMA OCCUPYING 1 OF 12 LYMPH NODES AND DEEPLY INVADING AXILLARY FAT  SEE NOTE  NOTE  The breast harbors a 1 5cm invasive carcinoma  and the axilla contains a 2 8cm nodule that probably represents a lymph node replaced by metastatic carcinoma  Blood vessels and lymphatic vessel invasion is not seen  The margins of the specimen appear free of the tumor  Diagnosis by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  09 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 09 04                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   THE TUMOR CELLS STAIN FOR ESTROGEN RECEPTOR PROTEIN  THE TUMOR CELLS STAIN FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN  SEE NOTE  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  14 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 08 56     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     THIS ADDENDUM HAS BEEN DELETED FOR THIS REPORT GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number only  is a modified radical mastectomy specimen measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 10 0cm  with an attached skin ellipse measuring REMOVED_DATE x 13 0cm  A nipple on the skin ellipse is grossly unremarkable  The axillary tail measures REMOVED_DATE x 10 0cm  Sectioning reveals a white firm mass located in the lateral part of the specimen at the beginning of the axillary tail measuring REMOVED_DATE x 2 8cm  The lesion approaches the deep resection margin which is the closest margin grossly to 1 0cm  Away from this area is a poorly difined  firm yellow white nodule  REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm  Section Code  A B  deep margins which are the closest margin  C D E  representative sections of the lesion  Two additional areas are submitted in cassettes O and P  respectively  A white  firm lesion measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm is submitted in cassettes Q T  The axillary tail measures REMOVED_DATE x 5 0cm  Representative sections of lymph node are submitted in cassettes G through N  Note that the largest lymph node which is bisected and submitted in half in cassette I measures REMOVED_DATE x 1 2cm  Resident  ALEXANDER KRATZ  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Charles Mason Ferguson  M D  PART   LEFT MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY   report_end  , Yes.
